WEBVTT

00:00:00.165 --> 00:00:04.062
{fluent}Hello, and a very warm welcome to Shah Spectrum.

00:00:04.062 --> 00:00:09.130
Pull up a chair, because today's story begins with something very ordinary:

00:00:09.130 --> 00:00:10.364
your electricity bill.

00:00:11.165 --> 00:00:17.269
On Thursday, American shares had one of their worst days in a long while.

00:00:17.269 --> 00:00:19.979
The Nasdaq fell by more than five per cent.

00:00:19.979 --> 00:00:23.592
The index of chip makers dropped by over twelve per cent.

00:00:23.592 --> 00:00:25.452
And the trigger was not a bad earnings report.

00:00:25.452 --> 00:00:29.420
It was an election result, and the power bills behind it.

00:00:30.193 --> 00:00:35.382
Hi, I'm Mittal Shah, and today I'll explain how a vote in America ended up shaking

00:00:35.382 --> 00:00:37.902
the biggest trade in the market:

00:00:37.902 --> 00:00:40.752
artificial intelligence.

00:00:40.752 --> 00:00:44.224
{fluent}First, the numbers, so we are clear about the day.

00:00:44.224 --> 00:00:49.361
At the close on Thursday, the Dow Jones fell by about eighteen hundred points, or

00:00:49.361 --> 00:00:50.631
three and a half per cent.

00:00:50.631 --> 00:00:55.627
The S&P 500, which tracks five hundred large American companies, fell by about four

00:00:55.627 --> 00:00:57.426
and a half per cent.

00:00:57.426 --> 00:01:02.902
The Nasdaq, heavy with technology firms, fell by more than five per cent, and ended

00:01:02.902 --> 00:01:07.031
at least ten per cent below its record close of the sixth of October.

00:01:07.031 --> 00:01:08.005
Traders call that a correction.

00:01:08.005 --> 00:01:12.168
Remember, just a day earlier, shares had rallied after the midterms.

00:01:12.991 --> 00:01:14.753
So what changed in a day?

00:01:14.753 --> 00:01:18.662
To understand, we need to look at what an AI data centre is.

00:01:18.662 --> 00:01:23.549
It is a giant warehouse of computers that train and run AI systems.

00:01:23.549 --> 00:01:28.488
Those computers need huge amounts of electricity, and they need it all day.

00:01:28.488 --> 00:01:33.562
As more of these centres are built, they compete with homes and businesses for

00:01:33.562 --> 00:01:35.623
power.

00:01:35.623 --> 00:01:41.591
In many places in America, electricity bills have been rising, and voters noticed.

00:01:41.591 --> 00:01:44.619
Data centres became a political issue.

00:01:45.375 --> 00:01:47.358
Now connect it to the election.

00:01:47.358 --> 00:01:50.054
Democrats won control of Congress.

00:01:50.054 --> 00:01:54.386
Some Democrats have called for restrictions, or pauses, on new data-centre projects.

00:01:54.386 --> 00:02:00.572
Earlier this year, New York already put a year-long pause on new very large

00:02:00.572 --> 00:02:05.050
facilities, and Pennsylvania requires developers to cover the cost of the

00:02:05.050 --> 00:02:06.395
infrastructure they need.

00:02:06.395 --> 00:02:08.590
Nothing has been passed in Congress.

00:02:08.590 --> 00:02:10.983
But investors began to wonder:

00:02:10.983 --> 00:02:16.643
what if building AI infrastructure becomes slower and more expensive?

00:02:17.427 --> 00:02:21.338
That worry matters because so much of the AI boom is built on one chain.

00:02:21.338 --> 00:02:23.587
Companies spend money on data centres.

00:02:23.587 --> 00:02:29.337
That money flows to chip makers, to power equipment suppliers, to the firms that run

00:02:29.337 --> 00:02:29.998
the buildings.

00:02:29.998 --> 00:02:32.489
If the spending slows, the whole chain feels it.

00:02:32.489 --> 00:02:35.861
One analyst described the market's thinking this way:

00:02:35.861 --> 00:02:41.217
the buildout becomes slower and more expensive, which rewrites growth expectations

00:02:41.217 --> 00:02:42.682
across the supply chain.

00:02:42.682 --> 00:02:47.077
And that is why the chip index fell harder than the rest of the market.

00:02:47.939 --> 00:02:49.207
Was the reaction fair?

00:02:49.207 --> 00:02:53.011
Even people inside the market are not sure.

00:02:53.011 --> 00:02:58.912
One fund manager told a news agency that this looked like the capitulation phase,

00:02:58.912 --> 00:03:04.708
meaning investors giving up and selling in a hurry, for shares tied to data-centre

00:03:04.708 --> 00:03:05.308
spending.

00:03:05.185 --> 00:03:07.929
He also said the size of the moves seemed outsized.

00:03:07.929 --> 00:03:08.718
In plain words:

00:03:08.718 --> 00:03:13.095
the fear is real, but the fall may be bigger than the facts so far support.

00:03:13.905 --> 00:03:15.049
What do we not know?

00:03:15.049 --> 00:03:19.394
Whether any limits on data centres will actually become law.

00:03:19.394 --> 00:03:22.494
How the president would respond to them.

00:03:22.494 --> 00:03:25.358
And whether companies will really slow down their building.

00:03:25.358 --> 00:03:30.390
Until those answers come, markets will keep moving on expectation, and expectations

00:03:30.390 --> 00:03:31.497
can change fast.

00:03:31.497 --> 00:03:32.688
{fluent}To sum up:

00:03:32.688 --> 00:03:34.529
nothing in the law has changed.

00:03:34.529 --> 00:03:40.490
What changed on Thursday was a belief, that politics could make the power-hungry AI

00:03:40.490 --> 00:03:41.945
buildout slower and costlier.

00:03:41.945 --> 00:03:46.546
One day it rallied, the next it sold off, which tells you how nervous this trade is.

00:03:47.283 --> 00:03:50.542
This is news and explanation, not investment advice.

00:03:51.326 --> 00:03:57.229
Before you go, we covered what the election result meant for oil, markets and India

00:03:57.229 --> 00:04:00.526
